Looking at the code using this, I think it will be a lot cleaner if this returned T and threw an exception if there was a problem.
That way, for example, processDeviceInterface would look like
```
  config.name = getValue<std::string>(configMap, "Name", config.name);
  config.address = getValue<uint64_t>(configMap, "Address", config.name);
  config.baudRate = getValue<uint64_t>(configMap, "BaudRate", config.name);
  config.portName = getValue<std::string>(configMap, "SerialPort", config.name);
  auto type = getValue<std::string>(configMap, "Type", config.name);
```
And we can in fact make the exception fall through and have processDeviceInterface (and other process* functions) return void and have the try catch in line 439 (function getConfigSubTree).
